The Geospatial Multi‑Physics Stratum Simulator (GMSS) is a unified planetary‑scale simulation architecture that couples astrophysical forcing, geological mechanics, multiphase subsurface flow, fracture dynamics, biogeochemical ecosystems, and cryptographic verification into a single governed computational framework.

GMSS integrates real astrophysical datasets as dynamic gravitational drivers, applies visco‑elasto‑plastic geomechanics (Biot consolidation), models multiphase fluid flow (H₂O, CH₄), simulates fracture mechanics via Mohr‑Coulomb yield, and embeds microbial and geochemical reaction‑diffusion systems. All state transitions are computed within a homomorphically encrypted kernel and validated using zero‑knowledge proofs, then written to a secure ledger.

If validated, GMSS represents a new class of scientific instrument: a cryptographically governed, multi‑domain Earth system engine capable of producing physically consistent, tamper‑proof planetary evolution histories.

